course: biology
lesson: 177
title: Vents and trenches: tubeworms, iron snails, the Challenger Deep and life without sunlight
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On the dark sea floor there are cracks where water, heated by hot rock deep below, gushes out full of minerals, and when it meets the freezing sea the minerals turn into black smoke and build tall chimneys.
explain: No sunlight ever reaches there, yet these hot vents are crowded with life: giant red-tipped tubeworms, hairy crabs, swarms of shrimp and even a snail wearing armour made of iron.
explain: The secret is bacteria that make food from chemicals in the vent water instead of from sunlight, so the whole town lives on chemical food, like a kitchen run on a gas pipe instead of a sunny window.
explain: Even deeper, in trenches such as the Challenger Deep, the water presses so hard it is like an elephant standing on your thumb, yet soft pale fish and little shrimp-like animals live there too.
fact: Hydrothermal vents are cracks in the sea floor where hot water full of dissolved minerals gushes out after being heated by hot rock below.
fact: Vent water can be hotter than three hundred degrees, but it does not boil because of the huge pressure of the deep sea.
fact: Giant tubeworms have no mouth and no gut; bacteria living inside them make food from chemicals in the vent water, and the worms can grow over two metres long.
fact: Yeti crabs, discovered in 2005, have hairy arms where they grow bacteria, and they wave their arms in the vent water to feed the bacteria and then eat them.
fact: The scaly-foot snail has a shell and scales made of iron sulfide, the only animal known to build armour from it, which may protect it from crabs and other predators.
fact: The Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench is the deepest point of the ocean, nearly eleven kilometres down, and two men first reached it in 1960 in the submersible (a small deep-diving craft) Trieste.
fact: Snailfish have soft bones and watery bodies, so the pressure does not crush them, and the deepest fish ever filmed was a snailfish at over eight kilometres.
fact: Amphipods are small shrimp-like animals in the deepest trenches that eat dead stuff sinking from above, and scientists have found plastic inside some of them.
fact: Before vents were found in 1977, scientists thought all life on Earth depended on sunlight; some scientists now think life on Earth may have started at vents, but nobody knows for sure.
fact: Cold seeps are places where gas and oil leak from the sea floor, and bacteria there make food from the chemicals; some seep animals live for hundreds of years.
fact: A deep-sea octopus was watched guarding her eggs for about four and a half years, the longest egg-guarding time known, because eggs grow very slowly in the cold.
fact: Vent shrimp crowd near the hot water but not too close, eat bacteria that grow on them, and have eye spots on their backs that may sense the dim glow of vents.
exercise: What is a hydrothermal vent? | A crack where hot, mineral-rich water gushes from the sea floor | A volcano that sticks out above the sea and smokes into the sky | A hole made by a whale | A pipe built by divers
exercise: Why does vent water not boil even at over three hundred degrees? | The huge pressure of the deep sea stops it | It is far too salty to boil | The cold sea all around cools it down at once | Water never boils in the sea
exercise: How do giant tubeworms get food without a mouth or gut? | Bacteria inside them make food from vent chemicals | They soak up sunlight through their bright red tips | They catch passing shrimp with their feathery tips | They eat the black smoke
exercise: Why does a yeti crab wave its hairy arms over a vent? | To feed the bacteria it grows there, which it then eats | To keep warm in the cold water | To scare away the tubeworms that want its spot on the chimney | To clean the chimney
exercise: When was the yeti crab discovered? | In 2005 | In 1905 | In 1805 | It has never been seen alive
exercise: What is special about the scaly-foot snail? | Its shell and scales are made of iron sulfide | It can climb out onto land and walk on the beach | Its shell is made of glass | It glows like a lantern
exercise: How deep is the Challenger Deep? | Nearly eleven kilometres | Only about one kilometre down | Only about a hundred metres | Over fifty kilometres, deeper than any mountain is high
exercise: When did people first reach the bottom of the Challenger Deep? | In 1960, in the submersible Trieste | In 1860, in a sailing ship | In 2019, for the very first time ever, by a robot | Nobody has ever reached it
exercise: Why does the huge pressure not crush a snailfish? | Its bones are soft and its body is full of water | It has a very thick iron shell, just like a submarine | It holds its breath | It swims up every night
exercise: What do amphipods in the deepest trenches eat? | Dead stuff sinking from above | Sunlit seaweed growing on the bottom | Tubeworms only, and nothing else | Nothing at all, they never eat
exercise: What did scientists find inside amphipods from the deepest trenches? | Plastic | Gold | Sunlight | Tiny pearls
exercise: What did scientists believe about life before vents were found in 1977? | That all life needed sunlight | That life needed only salt and water | That life first began on the Moon | That the deep sea was full of dinosaurs
exercise: What is a cold seep? | A place where gas and oil leak from the sea floor | A frozen waterfall under the sea | A whale's breathing hole | A cold current at the surface that flows from the poles
exercise: How long was a deep-sea octopus watched guarding her eggs? | About four and a half years | Only about four days, then they hatch | Only about four weeks in all | About four hundred years or more
exercise: Why do the octopus eggs take so long to hatch? | In the cold deep, eggs grow very slowly | The mother keeps forgetting them and swimming off | The eggs are made of hard stone, like pebbles | She keeps them in the hot water of a vent
exercise: What may the eye spots on a vent shrimp's back sense? | The dim glow from the vents | The light of the Sun shining down | The smell of food | The sound of whales singing far above
test: «tubeworms are plants innit, they're red and they grow» What is right? | No, they are animals that get food from bacteria inside them | Yes, they make all their own food from sunlight, just like grass | Yes, they are a kind of seaweed | No, they are a kind of rock
test: «vents r just underwater fire, nothing can live there» What is right? | Vents are hot water, not fire, and they are crowded with life | Right, nothing lives near them | Only plants live near them | Vents are frozen, not hot
test: «3 yeti crabs on a rock, each has 10 legs counting the claws, how many legs» | 30 | 13 | 20 | 33
test: «octopus mum and 2 octopus friends lol, how many arms» | 24 | 16 | 8 | 30
test: What would happen to the tubeworms if a vent stopped pouring out hot chemical water? | Their bacteria would have no chemicals, so the worms would starve | They would turn to sunlight instead | Nothing, they need nothing | They would let go and swim up to the surface to find new food there
test: In the cartoon SpongeBob, Bikini Bottom is full of sunlight and seaweed. Which is true of the deepest vents? | No sunlight reaches them, and life there runs on chemicals | They are lit by the Sun like a beach | They are full of grass | They are just as warm and bright as a rock pool
test: «mate says an elephant on ur thumb is nothing, the deep is easy» What is right? | The deepest trench presses about as hard as an elephant on your thumb, which is huge | The deep has no pressure at all | The deep presses less than air | Only a mouse on your thumb, which you could hardly feel down there at all
test: A cartoon fish at the bottom of the Mariana Trench has a hard armoured body. What does the real deepest fish have? | Soft bones and a watery body | A body made of solid iron plates | A huge heavy skull for strength | A body full of air, like a balloon
test: «who's the only animal with iron armour, a knight?» | The scaly-foot snail | The yeti crab | The giant tubeworm | The snailfish
test: «we found plastic at the bottom of the deepest trench? no way» What is right? | Yes, scientists found plastic inside amphipods there | No, plastic always floats at the top of the sea | No, nothing lives there to check, so nobody knows | Only in shallow seas
test: Why might some scientists think life on Earth started at vents? | Vents give energy from chemicals without needing sunlight | Vents are the warmest place on the surface of the Earth, warmer than deserts | Dinosaurs lived at vents | Vents are full of sunlight
exam: Identify the principal source of energy upon which the communities of hydrothermal vents depend. | Chemicals dissolved in the vent water, used by bacteria to manufacture food | Sunlight penetrating to the sea floor | The heat of the water consumed directly by the animals | Organic matter imported exclusively from the surface
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the giant tubeworm? | It possesses neither mouth nor gut and is nourished by internal bacteria | It photosynthesises by means of its red plumes | It is a variety of marine plant | It captures crabs with its plumes
exam: State the depth of the Challenger Deep and the year in which it was first attained by human beings. | Nearly eleven kilometres; first attained in 1960 | Nearly one kilometre; first attained in 1860 | Nearly fifty kilometres; never attained | Nearly eleven kilometres; first attained in 2019
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Mariana snailfish withstands the pressure prevailing at its depth. | Its skeleton is soft and its tissues are largely water | Its body is enclosed in armour of iron | It retains a large volume of air within its body | It ascends to shallow water daily
